[Event "Berlin: The Berlin Wall"]

{The Berlin (3...Nf6) is Kramnik's defence from 2000 against Kasparov. Black accepts an endgame where the king is in the centre but solid.}
1. e4 {Claims the centre: the pawn on e4 controls d5 and f5. It also opens a line for the queen on d1 and the bishop on f1.}
1... e5 {Claims the centre: the pawn on e5 controls d4 and f4. It also opens a line for the queen on d8 and the bishop on f8.}
2. Nf3 {Attacks e5. Develops the knight to f3, where it controls d4 and eyes the pawn on e5. That pawn is currently undefended.}
2... Nc6 {Develops the knight to c6, where it controls d4 and supports the pawn on e5.}
3. Bb5 {The Ruy Lopez. Develops the bishop to b5, where it controls c4 and eyes the knight on c6. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
3... Nf6 {[%cal Gf6e4] The Berlin. Develops the knight to f6, where it controls d5 and eyes the pawn on e4. That pawn is currently undefended.}
4. O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
4... Nxe4 {Takes the pawn on e4, and it cannot be taken back at once.}
5. d4 {Opens. Claims the centre: the pawn on d4 controls c5 and eyes the pawn on e5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c1 and the queen on d1.}
 (5. Re1 {Regains. The rook goes to e1 and eyes the knight on e4. That knight is currently undefended.}
5... Nd6 {Moves the knight out of attack to d6 and controls e4. It attacks the bishop on b5, which is undefended.}
6. Nxe5 {Takes the pawn on e5; Black will want to recapture.}
6... Be7 {Develops the bishop to e7, where it controls f6 and supports the knight on d6. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
7. Bf1 {Moves the bishop out of attack to f1.}
7... Nxe5 {Takes the knight on e5; White will want to recapture.}
8. Rxe5 {Recaptures on e5, restoring the material balance.}
8... O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
9. d4 {Claims the centre: the pawn on d4 controls c5 and supports the rook on e5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c1 and the queen on d1.}
9... Bf6 {Hits the rook. The bishop goes to f6 and eyes the rook on e5.}
10. Re1 {Moves the rook out of attack to e1 and controls e4 and e5.}
10... Re8 {The rook goes to e8 and controls e5 and e4. White's last move put a rook on e1, which this now pressures.}
)
5... Nd6 {Retreats with tempo. The knight goes to d6 and controls e4. It attacks the bishop on b5, which is undefended.}
6. Bxc6 {Takes the knight on c6; Black will want to recapture.}
6... dxc6 {Recaptures on c6, restoring the material balance.}
7. dxe5 {Takes the pawn on e5, and it cannot be taken back at once.}
7... Nf5 {Moves the knight out of attack to f5 and controls d4.}
8. Qxd8+ {Trades queens. The queen on d8 eyes the bishop on c8 and the pawn on c7 and controls d4 and d5. It is check, so the reply is forced.}
8... Kxd8 {Recaptures on d8, restoring the material balance.}
9. Nc3 {Develops the knight to c3, where it controls d5 and e4.}
9... Ke8 {Back home. The king goes to e8.}
10. h3 {Stops ...Ng3. The pawn on h3 controls g4.}
10... h5 {Gains space. The pawn on h5 takes g4 away from the opponent.} *

[Event "Berlin: Anti-Berlin 4.d3"]

{Against 4.d3, Black develops with ...Bc5.}
1. e4 {Claims the centre: the pawn on e4 controls d5 and f5. It also opens a line for the queen on d1 and the bishop on f1.}
1... e5 {Claims the centre: the pawn on e5 controls d4 and f4. It also opens a line for the queen on d8 and the bishop on f8.}
2. Nf3 {Attacks e5. Develops the knight to f3, where it controls d4 and eyes the pawn on e5. That pawn is currently undefended.}
2... Nc6 {Develops the knight to c6, where it controls d4 and supports the pawn on e5.}
3. Bb5 {Ruy Lopez. Develops the bishop to b5, where it controls c4 and eyes the knight on c6. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
3... Nf6 {Berlin. Develops the knight to f6, where it controls d5 and eyes the pawn on e4. That pawn is currently undefended.}
4. d3 {The Anti-Berlin. Supports the centre: the pawn on d3 controls c4 and supports the pawn on e4. It also opens a line for the bishop on c1.}
 (4. Nc3 {The Spanish Four Knights. Develops the knight to c3, where it controls d5 and supports the pawn on e4.}
4... Nd4 {[%cal Gd4b5] Counterattacks the b5-bishop. The knight goes to d4.}
)
4... Bc5 {Develops the bishop to c5, where it controls d4. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
 (4... d6 {Solid alternative. Supports the centre: the pawn on d6 controls c5 and supports the pawn on e5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c8.}
5. c3 {Prepares d4. Supports the centre: the pawn on c3 controls b4 and d4. It also opens a line for the queen on d1.}
5... g6 {[%csl Gg7] Fianchetto setup. The pawn on g6 controls f5 and h5.}
)
5. c3 {Prepares d4. Supports the centre: the pawn on c3 controls b4 and d4. It also opens a line for the queen on d1.}
5... O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
6. O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
6... d6 {Supports the centre: the pawn on d6 supports the bishop on c5 and the pawn on e5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c8.}
7. h3 {Stops ...Bg4. The pawn on h3 controls g4.} *
